    "evidence_text": "PLoS Med 4(9): e278.\n\nThe quality of modern cross-sectional ecologic studies: a bibliometric review.\n\nOpen Babel: An open chemical toolbox\n\nMAIN OUTCOME MEASURES Trial discontinuation before completion and non-publication after completion.\n\nDESIGN Randomized controlled trial.   \n\n\nSETTING A general medical journal.   \n\n\nRESULTS Reports were received from 221 reviewers (53%).\n\nSuch measures are unlikely to improve the quality of peer review reports.\n\nTwo also assessed decision-making.\n\nIt is not clear whether informed choice in screening affects uptake.\n\nOpen Access Increases Citation Rate\n\nIt is about citations.\n\nIt provides robust evidence that open-access articles (OA articles) are more immediately recognized and cited than non-OA articles.\n\nIt is therefore fitting that we publish such a paper.    \n\nThe analysis revealed that self-archived articles are also cited less often than OA articles from the same journal.    \n\nMoreover, the author of the article is also an editor of an open-access journal.\n\nPNAS was one of the first journals to offer an open-access option to its authors.\n\nThis means that similar experiments can be replicated.\n\nWe show that they provide effective and relatively powerful tests for evaluating the existence of such publication bias.",
